# Rover 10/25 six-light saloon, 1927-1933.
#
# The 10/25 was Rover's volume model of the late 1920s and early 1930s: a
# 1,185/1,389 cc four in a six-light saloon body, and the largest-selling
# missing Rover in the tree at 24,687 built.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rover_10
#
# Dimensions 3.962 x 1.575 x about 1.60 m (height estimated), wheelbase 2.642 m.
#
# Shape reference: a true level broadside of GK 5752. The rear three-quarter in
# the pack is a sports tourer with the hood erected, not a 10/25 saloon, and is
# used only for trim detail; the saloon tail follows the side view and the
# published dimensions.
#
# Colours: grade (d), no period UK document consulted. Black, Green, Grey and
# Maroon are period Rover finishes, recorded as plain names. Weights 12, 10, 9,
# 9, total 40, reflecting the model's volume, provisional pending the central
# rebase.
#
# Speed 95 km/h. Comfort 50/405, on the line 330 + 1.5 x 50. Classes 2,3.
# Dates mid-1927 to 1933 production, road tail staggered 1946-1948.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/rover-10.blend. Interchange:
# citycars/4.5/rover-10.dae. References: citycars/references/rover-10/
# (gitignored; Wikimedia Commons).
#
# Accepted deviations: the true side of GK 5752 governs. The rear three-quarter
# is a sports tourer with the hood erected, not a saloon, and is used only for
# trim detail; the saloon tail follows the side view and the published
# dimensions.


obj=citycar
name=Rover10(Black)
copyright=JamesPetts

DistributionWeight=12
intro_year=1927
intro_month=7
retire_year=1948
retire_month=6
speed=95
length=2
class=2,3
maximum_comfortable_journey_minutes=50
maximum_tolerable_journey_minutes=405

